# Local CI configuration (C++).
#
# Copy to .ci.env (which is gitignored) and edit what you need. This file is
# documentation: every knob below has a default in tools/ci.sh, so with no .ci.env at
# all the repo behaves exactly as described here.
#
#   cp .ci.env.example .ci.env

# Parallel build jobs for the build/asan/tsan/pristine stages.
CI_JOBS=4

# Build type for the normal, sanitizer and pristine builds.
CI_BUILD_TYPE=Debug

# Stages run when no stage names are given on the command line. All ten the kit ships
# for C++ (tidy joined the list on 2026-09-20; kitprobes on 2026-09-20 — a fix that must
# propagate ships a probe, card t_0cc793fb. See the notes at the end of this file).
CI_DEFAULT_STAGES="tree format kitprobes build tests version asan tsan tidy pristine"

# 1 = a missing tool (clang-format, clang-tidy) FAILS the run instead of SKIPping it.
# Set to 1 on a machine that is supposed to have them, so a silently missing gate
# cannot pass unnoticed.
CI_STRICT_TOOLS=0

# 1 = keep the pristine-checkout temp directory after a run (its path is printed).
CI_KEEP_TMP=0

# Where per-stage output goes. Gitignored; on failure the tail is printed and the path
# is named. Every one of these paths must be in .gitignore: the tree stage fails when
# the gate's own footprint would show up as an untracked file.
CI_LOG_DIR=.ci-logs
CI_BUILD_DIR=build
CI_ASAN_BUILD_DIR=build-asan
CI_TSAN_BUILD_DIR=build-tsan

# The source set the format and tidy stages own (a space-separated list of git pathspecs).
CI_SOURCE_GLOBS="'*.cpp' '*.cc' '*.cxx' '*.hpp' '*.hh' '*.h'"

# Extra -D flags for every configure in this script (dependencies, options, presets).
# CI_CMAKE_FLAGS="-DMYPROJECT_BUILD_TOOLS=ON"

# The test runner, given to `eval` with $CI_BUILD_DIR and $CI_JOBS expanded at run time.
# ctest is the portable default; a project that does not register tests with add_test()
# points this at its own suite instead.
#
# Computo used to narrow this with GTEST_FILTER: two wall-clock throughput assertions in
# test_thread_safety and two RSS-based leak heuristics in test_memory_safety could not pass
# on this machine. Nothing is filtered any more - the tests were fixed instead of hidden
# (see INCIDENTS.md) - so this is the kit's default value and `cp .ci.env.example .ci.env`
# still changes nothing but the file's presence.
CI_TEST_CMD="ctest --test-dir \$CI_BUILD_DIR --output-on-failure -j \$CI_JOBS"

# CI_ASAN_TEST_CMD is left unset deliberately. It exists for a test that genuinely cannot
# run under a sanitizer at all; here every case runs in every build. The one that used to
# need an exclusion - an RSS-growth leak heuristic that ASan's free() quarantine defeats -
# now detects the sanitizer and steps aside, because LeakSanitizer is the better leak check
# and the ASan stage runs with ASAN_OPTIONS=detect_leaks=1.

# Where the SECOND copy of the version number lives — the file the version stage
# compares against project(<name> VERSION x.y.z) in CMakeLists.txt. Two shapes are
# both fine:
#
#   generated by CMake (recommended):
#     # CMakeLists.txt
#     project(myapp VERSION 0.1.0 LANGUAGES CXX)
#     configure_file(cmake/version.hpp.in ${CMAKE_BINARY_DIR}/generated/version.hpp @ONLY)
#     # cmake/version.hpp.in
#     #pragma once
#     #define APP_VERSION "@PROJECT_VERSION@"
#
#   tracked by hand:  CI_VERSION_HEADER=include/myapp/version.hpp
CI_VERSION_HEADER="$CI_BUILD_DIR/generated/version.hpp"

# Binaries that must answer `--version` with the same number (a glob, expanded by the
# shell). Unset means the binaries are not asked — every project names them differently.
#
# Computo's CLI prints "Computo v<version>" (ArgumentParser::print_version) and the REPL
# banner prints "Computo REPL v<version>"; both read the generated header, so asking the
# binary is the last link in the chain.
CI_VERSION_BINARIES="\$CI_BUILD_DIR/computo"

# clang-tidy baseline — OPTIONAL. With no file, the tidy stage requires ZERO findings.
# Create one only to tolerate findings you inherited, and create it with the gate:
#
#   tools/ci.sh --write-tidy-baseline
#   git add .ci/tidy-baseline.txt && git commit -m "ci: accept the inherited clang-tidy findings"
#
# That one command runs the build stage (tidy refuses to run without
# $CI_BUILD_DIR/compile_commands.json — the build stage is what writes it), runs tidy, and
# writes every finding it reports into this file, then prints the `git add` line: the
# baseline is part of the repo, and the tree stage fails on a file that is neither
# committed nor ignored. Its tidy stage failing during that capture is expected — with no
# baseline yet, every inherited finding counts as new.
#
# Do NOT capture the file by hand from .ci-logs/tidy.log. The comparison normalises BOTH
# sides (tidy_key in tools/ci.sh): the repo root is stripped from every path, and
# `:line:column` is stripped, so one finding is still one finding after code moves above it,
# and a baseline captured in one clone still describes a checkout at another path (the
# nightly clean checkout, a colleague's machine). A raw copy of the log matches none of
# that, so every baselined finding reads as new and the tidy stage is permanently red — it
# cannot pass at all, on any machine. There is exactly one definition of the form, and it
# lives in the script; ask the script for the file.
#
# One honest consequence of the line-blind comparison: a SECOND identical finding in a file
# that already has one collapses into the first. Fix the baselined finding rather than
# growing the baseline around it.
CI_TIDY_BASELINE=.ci/tidy-baseline.txt

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# COMPUTO OVERRIDES — the knobs above that differ from the kit, and why
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The format and test-run overrides are GONE (2026-09-20). Both started as narrowings and
# had grown into permanent deviations of the gate; both are fixed at the source instead.
#
# CI_FORMAT_FALLBACK_HEAD (kit default 1 — no override here)
#   The format stage is branch-scoped: when the branch is level with origin/main it
#   re-checks the LAST COMMIT for clang-format drift. That fallback was red here because 31
#   of Computo's 54 committed source files pre-dated .clang-format — files nobody had
#   edited — so the override set it to 0 and the fallback never ran. Those 31 files are now
#   reformatted in one mechanical commit (a589d2b, proven formatting-only token by token),
#   so the kit's default is back and a level checkout checks something again. To keep
#   `git blame` readable across that commit:
#       git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# CI_TEST_CMD / CI_ASAN_TEST_CMD (above — the kit's default test command, no exclusions)
#   Both used to carry GTEST_FILTER names for four cases that could not pass here:
#     1. tests — ThreadSafetyTest.PerformanceUnderThreadLoad, "Single-threaded performance
#                too low" (EXPECT_GT(ops_per_second, 10000.0) vs 7174 ops/s measured).
#     2. tests — ThreadSafetyTest.HighConcurrencyStressTest, "Performance regression
#                detected" (same threshold; 6890 ops/s measured while a build ran).
#     3. asan  — MemorySafetyTest.LargeArrayMapOperation, "Potential memory leak detected:
#                179064 KB memory increase after test completion" (RSS heuristic vs the
#                ASan quarantine).
#     4. asan  — MemorySafetyTest.LargeArrayFilterOperation, same, 147152 KB.
#   (1) and (2) are now a liveness ceiling plus a scale-out floor, both with measured
#   headroom written at the assertion; (3) and (4) detect the sanitizer and step aside,
#   because the ASan stage runs LeakSanitizer with detect_leaks=1 and that is the real leak
#   check. Nothing is excluded from any build any more.
#
# CI_DEFAULT_STAGES (above) now includes tidy, and tidy is clean: zero findings and no
# baseline file. The three blockers it used to have are fixed in .clang-tidy, in the sources
# and in CMakeLists.txt - INCIDENTS.md carries the measurements:
#   1. HeaderFilterRegex matched the FetchContent'd JSOM headers under build/_deps (5178 of
#      5601 findings, 115 distinct, re-reported once per translation unit). It is a positive,
#      repo-scoped pattern now.
#   2. The check set is the value-only one JSOM and jsonTools use ('-*,bugprone-*,
#      -bugprone-easily-swappable-parameters,performance-*'); the 66 findings that survived
#      curation were FIXED, not baselined, so `.ci/tidy-baseline.txt` does not exist. If a
#      finding appears the stage fails - it never compares against a stored answer.
#   3. tests/test_json_pointer.cpp is in test_computo now, so the compile database covers
#      every source and the stage can certify a complete analysis.
# The two earlier overrides in this file (CI_FORMAT_FALLBACK_HEAD and the GTEST_FILTER test
# commands) are gone the same way. Nothing here hides a failure of CODE UNDER TEST any more.
#
# Run `tools/ci.sh --list` for the stages, `.githooks/` for the two callers, and
# `tools/ci.sh --help` for the options.
